headings:
 and, where the list needs
     one, a single explanatory sentence, then rows in the page's one row
     anatomy. Deliberately quieter than .set-head h2 — one card, one voice at
     the top of it — and separated by air plus a hairline rather than by a
     second border-and-shadow frame, which is what made three cards read as
     three unrelated features. */
  /* Same offset .set-card carries. These groups are REAL scroll targets, not
     just labels: crm.views redirects to #campaigns, #dismissed-proposals and
     #duplicates after every classify, restore, merge and undo, so a group
     landing under the sticky header would put the row you just acted on out
     of sight at the exact moment you look for confirmation. */
  .set-group { scroll-margin-top: 92px; }
  .set-group + .set-group { margin-top: var(--s5); padding-top: var(--s5);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .set-group-h { margin: 0; font-size: var(--fs-m); font-weight: var(--w-bold);
    color: var(--ink); }
  /* A sentence that belongs to a whole group, not to any one row. These used
     to be rendered AS rows — a .set-row holding a lone .set-row-desc with no
     label and no control — so the first thing in each of those three cards
     was a labelled row that labelled nothing and a hairline under prose. */
  .set-note { margin: var(--s2) 0 0; color: var(--ink-3);
    font-size: var(--fs-s); line-height: 1.6; max-width: 68ch; }
  /* The first row of a group is not :first-child (the heading and the note
     precede it), so it keeps the hairline from the base .set-row rule and the
     note reads as prose above a list rather than as the list's first item. */

  /* ---- Decisions rows: identity, state and evidence at three weights ----
     Every row in this card used to print its whole story — who it's about,
     what Networkly decided, and the forensic detail that decision rests on —
     as one paragraph of .set-row-desc, all the same small grey, so the
     evidence sentence ("Same mailbox name at two registered domains…") read
     at the same weight as the fact it was evidence FOR. Three tiers now:
     .set-row-label stays the identity (unchanged, page-wide); a state PILL
     rides beside it for the one-glance fact ("Your recruiting", a campaign
     kept or absorbed); and .dec-evidence holds the reasoning one tap away,
     the same <details> a reader never has to open to trust the row, exactly
     like every other progressive disclosure on this page. */
  /* The pill states this card's two classify-able facts. Same accent/neutral
     pairing .pill.plan-pro/.plan-free already established for Credits, reused
     rather than invented so a third color pair isn't added to the theme for
     what is the same kind of yes/no. */
  .pill.dec-yes { background: var(--accent-soft); color: var(--accent-ink); border-color: var(--accent-line); }
  .pill.dec-no  { background: var(--surface); color: var(--ink-2); border-color: var(--line-strong); }
  /* A quiet trailing fact riding on the SAME line as the identity it belongs
     to — an email address that disambiguates a name, or the other half of a
     merge pair. Regular weight and .ink-3 against the label's bold .ink, so
     the eye still lands on the identity first and reads this second. */
  .dec-sub { font-weight: var(--w-reg); color: var(--ink-3); font-size: var(--fs-s); }
  /* The evidence a merge suggestion or a completed merge rests on. It is a
     trust feature — it is WHY Networkly thinks two cards are one person —
     so it must stay reachable, but it is forensic detail, not the fact the
     row exists to state, so it does not compete at the row's own size and
     weight. <details> costs nothing closed and needs no script, the same
     contract every other disclosure on this page already relies on (see
     e.g. crm/_contact_live.html's coffee-chat brief). Real text in the
     summary, not CSS-generated content, for the same reason the segmented
     knob's "default" marker above is real text: it has to survive a screen
     reader and a stylesheet that failed to load. */
  /* A touch of left padding + a hairline rule, so this reads as subordinate
     to the row above it (evidence FOR the row's fact) rather than as a
     fourth line of the same paragraph — the one spot in an opened
     disclosure with no visual demarcation from its parent at all. */
  .dec-evidence { margin-top: var(--s3); padding-left: var(--s3);
    border-left: 2px solid var(--line); }
  .dec-evidence summary { cursor: pointer; color: var(--ink-3);
    font-size: var(--fs-xs); font-weight: var(--w-med); list-style: none;
    text-decoration: underline; text-decoration-style: dotted;
    text-underline-offset: 3px; }
  .dec-evidence summary::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }
  .dec-evidence summary:hover { color: var(--accent-ink); }
  .dec-evidence summary:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--accent);
    outline-offset: 2px; border-radius: var(--r-ctl); }
  .dec-evidence p { margin: var(--s1) 0 0; color: var(--ink-3);
    font-size: var(--fs-xs); line-height: 1.5; max-width: 60ch; }
  /* The click-through for already-decided items ("Past sends", "Past
     merges"). 2026-08-29: a settled campaign or a completed merge is a
     question already answered, and printing it inline at the same size as
     one still waiting on a tap was the exact "blatant" clutter the founder
     asked to remove. <details> again — costs nothing closed, needs no
     script — but this one carries a whole sublist rather than one sentence,
     so its summary reads as a compact section header, not a footnote. The
     ▸ is decoration, not data (unlike .dec-evidence's "Why" text above):
     the disclosure's own name, not CSS content, is what a screen reader or
     a broken stylesheet has to fall back on, and "Past sends (2)" already
     carries that on its own. */
  .dec-settled { margin-top: var(--s3); }
  .dec-settled summary { cursor: pointer; color: var(--ink-2);
    font-size: var(--fs-s); font-weight: var(--w-med); list-style: none;
    padding: var(--s2) 0; }
  .dec-settled summary::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }
  .dec-settled summary::before { content: "▸"; display: inline-block;
    margin-right: var(--s2); transition: transform var(--t-fast); }
  .dec-settled[open] summary::before { transform: rotate(90deg); }
  .dec-settled summary:hover { color: var(--accent-ink); }
  .dec-settled summary:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--accent);
    outline-offset: 2px; border-radius: var(--r-ctl); }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .dec-settled summary::before { transition: none; }
  }

  /* The two forms in Pace & Cadence share a card and must not run together.
     The pace form ends with its own inline Save; this hairline under it is
     what starts the cadence mechanism as a new thought, and it is the same
     hairline every .set-row boundary on the page already uses. */
  .set-form-lead { padding-bottom: var(--s5); margin-bottom: var(--s5);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .set-form-lead .pace-set { margin-bottom: 0; }
  .pace-inline .btn { min-height: 40px; }

  /* Visually hidden, still read aloud — the copy-button's live region. */
  .set-sr { position: absolute; width: 1px; height: 1px; padding: 0; margin: -1px;
    overflow: hidden; clip: rect(0 0 0 0); white-space: nowrap; border: 0; }

  @media (max-width: 820px) {
    .settings-wrap { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: var(--s4); }
    .settings-nav { position: static; flex-direction: row; flex-wrap: wrap;
      gap: var(--s1); padding-bottom: var(--s2); max-height: none;
      overflow: visible; padding-left: 0; }
    /* No vertical spine to ride when the nav is a horizontal row. */
    .settings-nav::before { display: none; }
    .settings-nav a::before { display: none; }
    .settings-nav a:hover { transform: none; }
    .settings-nav-title { display: none; }
    {% comment %}
    THE GROUP HEADERS STAY (2026-09-01). They used to be hidden here with the
    title, which turned the rail into eleven undifferentiated chips wrapped
    over three rows — and "Danger Zone" landed on row three beside "Profile",
    two items whose whole difference is that one of them deletes your
    account. The desktop rail separates them with a labelled group for
    exactly that reason; a phone does not need that distinction less.

    `flex-basis: 100%` is what makes it work in a wrapping flex row: the
    header claims a whole line, so the chips after it start a new row under
    their own label instead of trailing off the end of the previous group's.
    Four short mono lines against eleven chips whose grouping was otherwise
    guesswork.

    The rule the desktop version has (`::after`, a hairline to the right
    edge) goes: it was drawing a 200px line across a 375px screen to fill
    space beside a two-word label, which reads as a divider rather than as
    part of the label. The accent colour and the mono case carry it here.
    {% endcomment %}
    .settings-nav-group { flex-basis: 100%; margin: var(--s3) 0 2px;
      padding-left: 2px; font-size: var(--fs-micro); }
    .settings-nav-group:first-of-type { margin-top: 0; }
    .settings-nav-group::after { display: none; }
  }
  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .set-form .grid-2 { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    /* The two-up itself needs no rule here — auto-fit dropped it to one
       column long before 560px. Only its gap is worth tightening. */
    .set-grid-2 { gap: var(--s4); }
    /* Same reason .set-row stacks: a 12rem control reserved beside the label
       leaves a phone about 130px of label, and "Pre-Deadline Re-Ping" does
       not fit in 130px. Label, then input, then the prose — the desktop
       areas collapse to one column in that same order. */
    .set-field, .set-field.is-seg { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      grid-template-areas: "label" "control" "desc" "err";
      /* One column means nothing is spanning two rows, so the slack row the
         segmented field reserves on desktop has no job here. */
      grid-template-rows: none; }
    .set-field > .set-row-control { justify-self: start; }
    /* Stacked, the segment gets the full width like every other control. */
    .set-field.is-seg > .set-row-control { justify-self: stretch; }
    /* A phone gives the track about 200px, and three labels reading name AND
       number do not fit in 200px — they overlapped each other outright. The
       number is the part to drop: the sentence carries every one of them in
       full for anyone using a screen reader (see .cad-sentence's own
       comment -- it is visually hidden now, not on the page for a sighted
       phone user to fall back on either way), while the names are what make
       the dots mean anything. */
    .cad-rail { margin-left: 26px; margin-right: 26px; }
    .cad-lab u { display: none; }
    /* Stack label above control: at 375px a select or number input beside a
       long label gets crushed to unusable width. */
    .set-row { flex-direction: column; align-items: stretch; gap: var(--s3); }
    .set-row-control { justify-content: flex-start; flex-wrap: wrap; }
    /* A stacked row's BUTTON stretches to the row's full width — centred by
       construction, and a bigger tap target where taps are the input. Left
       hanging at flex-start it floated in dead space (reported against the
       Danger Zone's Delete Account, and true of every stacked row's button).
       Buttons only: a select or toggle at full width reads as a text field. */
    .set-row-control .btn { flex: 1 1 auto; text-align: center; justify-content: center; }
    /* ...AND THE RULE ABOVE COULD NOT SEE HALF OF THEM. `flex` only means
       anything to a flex item, i.e. to a direct child of a flex box. A row
       whose action POSTs wraps its button in a <form>, so the button's flex
       container is that form and not `.set-row-control` — the declaration
       computed on every button (`flex: 1 1 auto` on all eleven this deploy
       renders, measured) and did nothing on the wrapped ones, because the
       form itself was the unstretched item.

       Measured at 375px in a 301px control track: Manage, Change, Sign Out
       and Delete Account filled it at 301px, while Reset (Calendar Link)
       sat at 77px and the Decisions Yes/No pair at 64px and 59px — the same
       "floated in dead space" defect the comment above says was reported
       against Delete Account and fixed, still live in the three rows the
       selector could not reach. Whether an action needs a POST is invisible
       to the reader, so it must not be what decides how the button looks.

       The form becomes the stretching item and a flex box itself, which
       hands the stretch straight through to the button already asking for
       it. Two forms in one row (Decisions' Yes/No) split the track evenly,
       exactly as the two plain buttons of Import/Export already do. */
    .set-row-control > form { flex: 1 1 auto; display: flex; }
    .set-row-value { align-self: flex-start; }
  }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { html { scroll-behavior: auto; } }

  /* ---- The pace ring: setting and readout as the same shape -------------
     The SVG is a control (drag or click the ring), but the number input
     remains the accessible one — the ring writes to it and it writes to the
     ring, so either hand works. --p is the fill fraction. */
  .pace-set { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: var(--s5); flex-wrap: wrap;
    margin-bottom: var(--s3); }
  .pace-main { flex: 1 1 240px; min-width: 0; display: flex;
    flex-direction: column; gap: var(--s2); }
  .pace-inline { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: var(--s4); flex-wrap: wrap;
    margin-top: var(--s1); }
  .pace-inline .pace-read { margin: 0; }
  .pace-ring-widget { position: relative; width: 132px; height: 132px;
    cursor: pointer; touch-action: none; flex: none; }
  .pace-ring-widget svg { width: 100%; height: 100%; transform: rotate(-90deg); }
  .pace-ring-widget .pr-track { fill: none; stroke: var(--line); stroke-width: 9; }
  .pace-ring-widget .pr-fill { fill: none; stroke: var(--accent); stroke-width: 9;
    stroke-linecap: round; stroke-dasharray: 339.3;
    stroke-dashoffset: calc(339.3 * (1 - var(--p, 0)));
    transition: stroke-dashoffset 320ms cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1); }
  .pace-ring-widget .pr-val { position: absolute; inset: 0; display: grid;
    place-content: center; text-align: center; }
  /* `--fs-figure`, the same size `.set-row-value` takes two cards up. Both
     are a number inside a panel, which is the whole distinction the figure
     token draws: a display size is for a TITLE. It read 30px against that
     card's 24px before the scale landed, which was two answers to one
     question sitting a scroll apart. */
  .pace-ring-widget .pr-val b { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--fs-figure);
    font-weight: var(--w-med); color: var(--accent-ink); line-height: 1; }
  .pace-ring-widget .pr-val span { font-size: var(--fs-nano); text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em; color: var(--ink-3); }
  .pace-read { font-size: var(--fs-s); color: var(--ink-2); max-width: 26rem; }
  .pace-read b { color: var(--accent-ink); }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { .pace-ring-widget .pr-fill { transition: none; } }

  /* The theme picker: three segments, the chosen one filled. Buttons rather
     than radios because the state lives in localStorage, not in the form
     that posts to the server — role="radiogroup" carries the semantics the
     markup no longer implies. */
  .theme-pick { display: inline-flex; border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: 999px; overflow: hidden; background: var(--surface); }
  .theme-opt { border: 0; background: none; cursor: pointer;
    padding: 8px 16px; min-height: 40px; font-size: var(--fs-s);
    font-weight: var(--w-med); color: var(--ink-2);
    transition: background var(--t-fast), color var(--t-fast); }
  .theme-opt + .theme-opt { border-left: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .theme-opt:hover { background: var(--accent-soft); color: var(--accent-ink); }
  .theme-opt.is-on { background: var(--accent); color: var(--on-accent); }

  /* ---- Target Firms: the tier board, editable from Settings -----------
     Same drag-to-retier mechanism the Network page's board already uses
     (same endpoint, same event shape) — a firm dragged here and a firm
     dragged there write through one code path, so the two boards can
     never disagree about a person's tier. What Settings adds is what the
     Network board can't do: START and STOP tracking a firm at all. */
  /* One firm's whole row, at one-per-row, is the shape the Network board
     avoided on purpose (see .tier-grid's own note: "bounded (~2 rows) so
     a tier with many firms doesn't stretch the whole panel") — a first
     pass here missed that and put 69 firms in 69 full-width rows across
     three side-by-side columns, taller than the rest of Settings
     combined. Same fix as Network's: firms are TAGS, not cards, so they
     wrap several to a row, and each tier is a fixed peek height with its
     own scrollbar rather than growing the page. Stacked sections instead
     of 3 side-by-side columns for the same reason — a 190px-wide column
     of chips wraps at most one or two per row and buys nothing. */
  .tf-board { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--s3);
    margin: var(--s3) 0 var(--s5); }
  .tf-col { border-left-color: var(--line-strong);
    padding: var(--s3) var(--s4);
    transition: border-color var(--t-fast), background-color var(--t-fast); }
  /* Escalating emphasis, not a severity colour (the Relationship gaps strip's
     tier borders mean "how urgent" — reusing that red/amber/grey here
     would say something is WRONG with Tier 1, when it's simply the most
     important section). Accent scales down tier by tier instead. */
  .tf-col[data-tier="1"] { border-left-color: var(--accent); }
  .tf-col[data-tier="2"] { border-left-color: var(--accent-line); }
  .tf-col[data-tier="3"] { border-left-color: var(--line-strong); }
  .tf-col.drop-hot { background: var(--accent-soft); border-color: var(--accent-line); }
  .tf-col-title { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: var(--s2);
    margin: 0 0 var(--s2); font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--fs-xs);
    letter-spacing: 0.06em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-3); }
  /* A counted pill, not a bare numeral trailing the title: "TIER 1 15" read
     as one number ("tier one fifteen") at this size and letter-spacing. */
  .tf-col-count { font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; color: var(--ink-2);
    background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: 999px; padding: 0 6px; letter-spacing: 0; }
  /* ~3 rows before it scrolls internally, same "bounded peek, not an
     unbounded list" contract as .tier-grid and .tf-results below. */
  .tf-chips { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-content: flex-start;
    gap: var(--s2); max-height: 152px; overflow-y: auto; padding-right: 4px;
    scrollbar-width: thin; scrollbar-color: var(--line-strong) transparent; }
  .tf-chips::-webkit-scrollbar { width: 8px; }
  .tf-chips::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb { background: var(--line-strong); border-radius: 999px; }
  .tf-chip { flex: none; display: flex; align-items: center;
    gap: 6px; max-width: 250px; padding: 6px 6px 6px 13px; border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: 999px; background: var(--paper); cursor: grab;
    font-size: var(--fs-s); font-weight: var(--w-med); color: var(--ink);
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-1); animation: chip-check 260ms cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1) both;
    transition: border-color var(--t-fast), box-shadow var(--t-med), opacity var(--t-med), transform var(--t-med); }
  .tf-chip span { min-width: 0; overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis; white-space: nowrap; }
  .tf-chip:hover { border-color: var(--accent-line); box-shadow: var(--shadow-2); }
  .tf-chip.dragging { opacity: 0.5; cursor: grabbing; }
  .tf-chip.is-leaving { opacity: 0; transform: scale(0.85); }
  /* Tier controls use the shared select enhancement. Remove stays visible. */
  .tf-chip-x { flex: none; display: inline-flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
    width: 18px; height: 18px; border: 0; border-radius: 50%; background: transparent;
    color: var(--ink-3); cursor: pointer;
    opacity: 0.4;
    transition: background-color var(--t-fast), color var(--t-fast), opacity var(--t-fast); }
  .tf-chip-x svg { width: 10px; height: 10px; pointer-events: none; }
  .tf-chip:hover .tf-chip-x, .tf-chip:focus-within .tf-chip-x { opacity: 1; }
  .tf-chip-x:hover { background: var(--danger-soft); color: var(--danger); }
  .tf-chip-x:focus-visible { opacity: 1; outline: 2px solid var(--accent); outline-offset: 1px; }
  @media (hover: none) {
    .tf-chip-x { opacity: 1; }
  }
  .tf-empty { margin: 0; color: var(--ink-3); font-size: var(--fs-xs); font-style: italic; }

  .tf-add label { display: block; margin: 0 0 var(--s2); }
  .tf-add input[type="text"] { max-width: 26rem; }
  .tf-results { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 2px; margin-top: var(--s3);
    max-height: 260px; overflow-y: auto; border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--r-panel); padding: var(--s1); }
  .tf-result { display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--s3); padding: 6px 10px; border-radius: var(--r-ctl);
    font-size: var(--fs-s); color: var(--ink-2); }
  .tf-result:hover { background: var(--accent-soft); }
  .tf-result.is-hidden { display: none; }
  /* The regroup heading above (`matching_untracked_firms` /
     `other_untracked_firms`) — same convention as onboarding's
     `.ob-firms-heading`. */
  .tf-group-heading { margin: var(--s2) 0 2px; padding: 0 10px;
    font-size: var(--fs-xs); font-weight: var(--w-med); color: var(--ink-3);
    text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.05em; }
  .tf-group-heading:first-child { margin-top: 0; }
  .tf-add-btns { flex: none; display: flex; gap: 6px; }
  {% comment %}
  `--r-ctl`. "Tier 1 / Tier 2 / Tier 3" beside a search result is a row of
  three buttons that each add a firm — pressable, so squared, per the
  control-shape rule in networkly.css §6. It read as a chip and it is not one:
  a chip reports a state, and these do something. They were also the odd
  shape out on a page that already carried nine button treatments.

  Not a segmented choice either, which is the rule's one exception: the three
  are alternative destinations for one firm, not a persistent selection with
  a current member, and nothing here stays chosen after the click.
  {% endcomment %}
  .tf-add-btn { border: 1px solid var(--line); background: var(--surface);
    color: var(--ink-2); border-radius: var(--r-ctl); padding: 3px 11px;
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 11px; font-weight: var(--w-med);
    cursor: pointer; transition: border-color var(--t-fast), background-color var(--t-fast),
      color var(--t-fast), transform var(--t-fast); }
  .tf-add-btn:hover { border-color: var(--accent); background: var(--accent-soft);
    color: var(--accent-ink); transform: translateY(-1px); }
  .tf-add-btn:active { transform: translateY(0) scale(0.96); }
  .tf-empty-search { margin: var(--s2) 0 0; color: var(--ink-3); font-size: var(--fs-s); font-style: italic; }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .tf-chip { animation: none; }
    .tf-add-btn:hover { transform: none; }
  }
</style>
{% endblock %}

{% block content %}
{% comment %}
hx-boost, not per-form hx-post/hx-target: every action on this page except
Profile (which already has its own hx-post/hx-target and is untouched by
this) was a plain <form method="post">, so saving Cadence or restoring a
dismissed contact did a full browser navigation -- a white-flash reload
that reset scroll position and, combined with base.html ALSO rendering
`messages` globally (see below), showed the flash message twice: once from
this page's own render, once from base.html's. Boosting intercepts every
plain <form>/<a> under this element and turns it into the same request,
same response, same redirect-following behaviour, just fetched over AJAX
and swapped into <body> instead of a hard navigation -- no view on either
app had to change. An element with its OWN hx-* attributes (Profile's
form) is untouched by an ancestor's hx-boost; htmx only boosts elements
that don't already declare explicit htmx behaviour.
{% endcomment %}
<div class="page support-account support-settings" hx-boost="true">
  {% include "_pagehead.html" with title="Settings" sub="Manage your profile, target firms, and account." %}
  {% comment %}
  Messages render ONCE, here, not a second time on this page -- see
  base.html's own {% templatetag openblock %} if messages {% templatetag closeblock %}
  block for why duplicating that loop here showed every flash message
  twice.
  {% endcomment %}

  <div class="settings-wrap">
    {% comment %}
    Rail groups follow the pattern every comparable product converged on
    (LinkedIn, Notion, Linear): "who you are" and "how the product behaves"
    are separated from "how you get in and what we hold on you".

    THE RAIL AND THE PAGE ARE THE SAME LIST, IN THE SAME ORDER. They were not:
    the rail read Profile, Work Authorization, Appearance, Target Firms while
